  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The equatorial diameter exceeds the polar diameter, so the earth bulges at the equator.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    12 756 km at the equator; the polar diameter is 42 km shorter.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    12 714 km, which is about 42 km less than the equatorial diameter.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    40 075 km round the widest part of the earth.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The curve of the surface hides the hull before the mast, which a flat earth would not do.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The shadow is always circular, whatever the earth's orientation.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One rotation takes 24 hours, which is the length of the day.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Rotation is west to east, which is why the sun appears to rise in the east.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Day and night follow from rotation; seasons and day length follow from revolution and tilt.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    365¼ days — the quarter is why a leap day is added every four years.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The axis is tilted 23½° and keeps its direction, so each hemisphere leans towards the sun in turn.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The axis is tilted 23½° from the perpendicular to the plane of orbit.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    21 March is the vernal equinox, when the sun is over the equator and day and night are equal.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The June solstice puts the overhead sun at 23½°N, the Tropic of Cancer.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The December solstice puts the overhead sun at 23½°S, the Tropic of Capricorn.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Latitude is angular distance north or south of the equator, 0° to 90°.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Longitude is angular distance east or west of Greenwich, 0° to 180°.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Latitude runs to 90° at each pole; longitude runs to 180°.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Longitude is measured to 180° east and 180° west of Greenwich.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    About 111 km everywhere, because the parallels are evenly spaced.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    360° in 24 hours gives 15° per hour.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    15° = 60 minutes, so 1° = 4 minutes.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    30° ÷ 15 = 2 hours; east is ahead, so the time is 2.00 p.m.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    30° ÷ 15 = 2 hours; west is behind, so the time is 10.00 a.m.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The date changes at about 180°, drawn in a zigzag to avoid cutting through countries.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Crossing westward, the traveller moves into a day that has already begun, so a day is added.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    23½° north of the equator, the latitude reached by the overhead sun in June.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    66½° north, the latitude beyond which the sun does not set in midsummer.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The torrid (tropical) zone lies between the tropics and receives the overhead sun.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Inside the polar circles the sun stays above the horizon for part of the summer.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Deflection is to the right in the northern hemisphere and to the left in the southern.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The equator is bisected by the circle of illumination every day of the year.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The sun's apparent path stays within 23½° of the equator, so it can be overhead only between the tropics.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Perihelion occurs in early January, which is why distance cannot explain the northern winter.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The equator is a great circle; other parallels are small circles.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Meridians run from pole to pole and meet at the poles; parallels never meet.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Parallels are parallel to one another; meridians converge at the poles.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    West Africa Time is GMT + 1, based on the 15°E meridian.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The seasons follow from revolution and the tilt of the axis, not from rotation.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The earth rotates west to east, so the sun appears to travel east to west.
  ]

]

